#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120595} :activity/id #uuid "9a213bfe-4551-5c56-be1e-798baa94efb6" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048339 :topic/id #uuid "96c0d2ef-1891-5083-879c-8dcc09b70542" :topic/math-academy-id 1834} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120595 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121008 :tutorial/content "The **osculating circle** (sometimes called the *kissing circle*) of a curve $C$ at a point $P$ is a circle that\n\n- is tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P,$ and\n\n- has the same curvature as the curve $C$ at the point $P.$\n\nTo demonstrate, consider the curve $C$ given by the vector function\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = \\langle t+1, 2-t^2\\rangle.\n$$\n\nThe curve $C$ and its osculating circle at the point $P$ are shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/f5/f51c1d1f45f2484991c28296d9b9ceeda134965993d130c2611c0e4bfb4ab32b.png)\n\nAt the point $P(1,2),$ where $t=0,$ the curvature is $\\kappa(0)=2.$\n\nIt can be proved that any circle has constant curvature, which is equal to the reciprocal of the circle's radius. So, for a general curve $\\mathbf r (t)$ we define its \n **radius of curvature** as\n\n$$\nR(t) = \\dfrac 1 {\\kappa (t)}. % = \\dfrac{\\left\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\right\\|}{\\| \\mathbf T'(t) \\|}\n$$\n\nIn our example above, we get\n\n$$\nR(0) = \\dfrac 1 {\\kappa (0)} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** Since computing the radius of curvature requires computing the curvature first, we might use different formulas for curvature depending on the situation.\n\nFor example, if a curve is parametrized by its arc length, we use the formula\n\n$$\nR(s) = \\dfrac 1 {\\kappa (s)} = \\dfrac{1}{\\| \\mathbf{T}'(s) \\|}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, if a curve uses a general parameter $t,$ we use the formula\n\n$$\nR(t) = \\dfrac 1 {\\kappa (t)} = \\dfrac{\\left\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\right\\|}{\\| \\mathbf T'(t) \\|}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "b58176ee-322d-5b4a-aa8c-e9972cbe1229" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2488 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "812b9e66-1124-5116-9356-ca760a8dde07" :step/math-academy-id 11116 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120596}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120596 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119758 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119574 :question/id #uuid "3e36e916-16af-55eb-9ece-026b68eb93ea" :question/math-academy-id "e-3338"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4d622a24-2705-53bb-a07f-8cc11775cf09" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049196}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120322} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120353} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235465} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266134} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266135} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266136} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335739} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335740}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Radius of Curvature"} :step/id #uuid "869f0a40-96aa-5ddd-b227-5006ff45ea4d" :step/math-academy-id 11118 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120597}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120597 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121009 :tutorial/content "The center of the osculating circle for a curve $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ at a point where $t=t_0$ is located at the tip of the vector \n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t_0)+R(t_0) \\mathbf N(t_0)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf N(t)$ denotes the principal normal vector to the curve.\n\nFor example, the curve $C$ parameterized by the vector function \n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = \\bigg\\langle t+1, 2- \\dfrac{t^2}{2} \\bigg\\rangle\n$$\n\nhas the normal vector\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{N}(t) = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{1+t^2}} \\langle -t, \\: -1 \\rangle ,\n$$\n\nand the radius of curvature at the point $P(1,2)$ where $t=0$ is $R(0)=1.$\n\nSo, the center of the osculating circle is at the tip of the vector\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0)+R(0)N(0) & =⟨0+1,\\,2-\\frac{{0}^{2}}{2}⟩+1\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{0}^{2}}}⟨0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,2⟩+⟨0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the center of the osculating circle at the point where $t=0$ is located at $\\left(1, 1 \\right),$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/d8/d8cd8407e6398b3409e7f575e152976669393914e2e5d7335121287276af6e41.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "5a31a99b-6c09-5d1c-ad68-7b5f38c17efb" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2489 :tutorial/title "The Center of the Osculating Circle"} :step/id #uuid "88a27337-45d9-59ab-ac93-024d63adfb66" :step/math-academy-id 11120 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120598}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120598 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119759 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119575 :question/id #uuid "7f4861fe-a783-5874-a22b-b4644257ae0f" :question/math-academy-id "e-3339"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f6e70249-58d6-57ce-867c-ff9a3b4219f4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048616}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120354} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120498} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266148} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266149} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266150} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268851} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362218} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362219} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392898} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400653} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404933}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Center of the Osculating Circle"} :step/id #uuid "4edcd617-8c81-5a66-b0c0-b9510d42e399" :step/math-academy-id 11121 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120600}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120599 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121010 :tutorial/content "If a curve has a curvature of zero at some point, the radius of curvature at that point is defined to be infinite.\n\nIt follows from the definition that a straight line has zero curvature at all of its points. Conversely, if a curve has zero curvature at any point, it must be contained on a straight line.\n\nSuppose now that we want to find the radius of curvature for the curve \n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = \\left\\langle t^2, \\: 1 - t^2, \\: 1+ t^2 \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nat an arbitrary point where $t> 0.$\n\nFirst, we calculate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and its magnitude $\\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\|\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t,\\,-2t,\\,2t⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(2t)^{2}+(-2t)^{2}+(2t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{12{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that in the last step, we used the fact that $\\sqrt{t^2}=t$ since $t>0.$\n\nNext, we find the unit tangent and its derivative for $t>0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{{r}^{'}(t)}{‖{r}^{'}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{3}t}\\cdot ⟨2t,\\,-2t,\\,2t⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}⟨1,-1,1⟩ \\\\ {T}^{'}(t) & =⟨0,0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the radius of curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(t) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{'}(t)‖}{‖{T}^{'}(t)‖}=\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}t}{0}=\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, despite its \"parabolic\" appearance, the given curve is contained on a straight line!\n\n**Note:** Often, the unit tangent vector of a curve $\\mathbf T(t)$ may look a bit complicated. In such cases, the following formula involving a cross product may be useful. It says that the radius of curvature of a twice-differentiable curve $\\mathbf r(t)$ is given by \n\n$$\nR(t)=  \\dfrac{1}{\\kappa(t)} =\\frac{{{{\\left\\| {\\mathbf r'\\left( t \\right)} \\right\\|}^3}}}{{\\left\\| {\\mathbf r'\\left( t \\right) \\times \\mathbf r''\\left( t \\right)} \\right\\|}}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "590485ba-30a6-549b-a4cf-d6641c92a033" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2490 :tutorial/title "Infinite Radius of Curvature"} :step/id #uuid "5b0a8082-2fe6-5852-b950-a17b2bc3d3ca" :step/math-academy-id 11122} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120600 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119760 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119576 :question/id #uuid "8d58f131-7260-5346-9a88-60862c0a1b55" :question/math-academy-id "e-3340"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a053eb10-be16-5423-9b7b-c23677bb6335" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048648}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120381} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120558} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266137} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266138} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266139} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266140} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266141} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320790} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362307} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362308} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362317}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Radius of Curvature Using the Cross Product Formula"} :step/id #uuid "25d03bd3-ad37-5232-b295-cdb7c4d45fa8" :step/math-academy-id 11123 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120599}}] :activity/title "Radius of Curvature" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186120034}]}
